From: moWerk <mo@mowerk.net>
Subject: [PATCH] sailfish_wifi: autoscan hold-off as a plain timer

The autoscan hold-off only rate-limits scan requests while the system is
awake, but it was armed as a wakeup timer. On a phone that suspends 5 s
after each wakeup (mce autosleep) the 5 s hold-off expired just after the
system had suspended again, iphb rounded the wakeup up to its 30 s grain,
and the phone woke about a minute later; the expiry could start the next
scan, which armed the next hold-off. Seen on the F(x)tec Pro1-X (mainline,
SailfishOS 5.2) as a wakeup every 54-59 s from connmand in dsme's iphb
log. A plain GLib timer does not run while the system sleeps.

---
diff --git a/connman/plugins/sailfish_wifi.c b/connman/plugins/sailfish_wifi.c
index f3da326a..438e909c 100644
--- a/connman/plugins/sailfish_wifi.c
+++ b/connman/plugins/sailfish_wifi.c
@@ -2931,8 +2931,16 @@ static void wifi_device_autoscan_schedule_next(struct wifi_device *dev)
 	if (dev->autoscan_holdoff_timer_id) {
 		g_source_remove(dev->autoscan_holdoff_timer_id);
 	}
+	/*
+	 * A plain timer, not a wakeup timer: the hold-off only rate-limits
+	 * scans while the system is awake. As a wakeup timer it woke a
+	 * suspended phone about a minute later (the hold-off expired 5 s after
+	 * a wakeup, just after the system had suspended again, and iphb's 30 s
+	 * grain rounded the wakeup up), and the expiry could start the next
+	 * scan, which armed the next hold-off: a wakeup every minute.
+	 */
 	dev->autoscan_holdoff_timer_id =
-		connman_wakeup_timer_add_seconds(WIFI_AUTOSCAN_MIN_SEC,
+		g_timeout_add_seconds(WIFI_AUTOSCAN_MIN_SEC,
 			wifi_device_autoscan_holdoff_timer_expired, dev);
 
 	/* Schedule the next scan */
